ounds like deferral of  responsibility and accountability  rather than a collaborative approach  between  government to nation  the federal government is poised to  break its promise to end all boil water  advisories in first nations communities  by march a joint investigation with  global news reveals  allegations of incomplete and faulty  work plus  delays and overcharges those are some of  the concerns from first nations  communities  that we took to the minister responsible  for indigenous services  again here's christa hesse in an  interview  indigenous services minister mark miller  wouldn't comment on specific contractors  but said the findings of our  investigation were startling  the reporting that you and others have  done was certainly alarming  issues with kingdom were flagged as  early as 2010 yet the government awarded  the company six more major water  projects  after that why did that happen these are  our decisions that are taken with the  community  we do our best not to force any  particular community into a particular  path  it sounds like deferral of  responsibility and accountability  rather than a collaborative approach  between  government to nation according to  indigenous services canada's guidelines  first nations should award the contract  to the lowest acceptable bidder  provided that the contractor's tender is  complete and they appear capable of  doing the work  the department spokesperson said this  policy follows industry standards  does it follow industry standards yes it  follows historic practice  but it's not the best practice minister  miller has admitted that problems with  contractors are widespread  yet indigenous services canada does not  currently have a system to track  complaints and bad companies  that work on first nation water projects  the fact that they would claim that they  don't bother to check whether or not the  job gets done right  speaks to the to the systemic negligence  indigenous services canada will put  their check mark and say oh  that water treatment plan was replaced  you no longer need funding for the next  x amount of years  and who pays a consequence is all the  members of that community  we asked minister miller if he would  commit to tracking complaints about  contractors  going forward i'm keen to have as much  data  as possible and if it's helpful in  ensuring that  the people aren't the subject of sharp  practices i'm glad to do that  members of nishkaniga first nation have  now been living in a hotel in thunder  bay  for two months cases of coven 19 have  been on the rise  as the country grapples with the second  wave and residents  are worried marissa saccone is a mother  of two  who had to evacuate with her daughters  their girls picked a spot  back home when they said that we were  getting evacuated i said  no of course my partner talked me out of  it  marcus muniz marisa's partner is one of  the water operators in nishkanaga and  had to stay behind  she says her eldest daughter maya has  been struggling she had her  like her face on her knees  like she was sad so i asked her  to tell me how she feels and then she  says  she feels trapped marissa is 25 years  old and has never had access to clean  drinking water at home  she says just to prepare a bath can be a  two to three hour ordeal  it's very difficult carrying four five  five containers of ten liter water  taking it all the way to your house the  government advises people living under a  boil water advisory to not be  children or elders in the tap water but  often  people have no choice the only access to  clean drinking water in the community  is a reverse osmosis machine located in  a tiny shed on the edge of the reserve  and it often breaks down marissa says  her daughters have suffered from skin  rashes and sores  and that the outbreaks have caused her  to sometimes be scared to bathe them at  all  but despite these challenges she wants  to go home  even if the boil water advisory is still  in effect  we did it before so  we could do it again  i got a pack just a week before  christmas the community got news it  would be able to return to niskanega  finally it was time to go home  the water is running again but the  community's problems are far from  solved the boil water advisory is still  in effect  and work on the treatment plant has  slowed because of the pandemic  so for now they will continue to rely on  shipments of bottled water  the federal government has agreed to  investigate the contractors that worked  on niskanega's water system  and is working with the community on a  long-term plan to lift the boil water  advisory  but chief munius says he believes it'll  take an entire generation  to heal people's distrust for the water  we need to  work on the the trauma that they faced  over  over 25 years nobody can say  he said no you trust the water now  nobody can say that to them the federal  government has paid kingdom  nearly two million dollars to settle a  legal claim launched by the company over  its cancelled contract  the consequences of the low-bid system  and lack of oversight stretch far beyond  one contractor  and three communities and these are just  some of the challenges contributing to  water insecurity on first nations  some communities lack pipes to connect  treatment plans to homes  while others have trouble retaining  certified water operators  but as our reporting further illustrates  what connects all of these chronic  issues  are the colonial structures in place  that impede the development of these  communities  and the delivery of basic services like  clean drinking water  we're still thought of as less than  human  as objects as things as a problem as an  issue that can be swept aside and but  you put them in the reserves and then  you can just ignore it  but we can't be ignored we are people  and we are people that live  within this country a nation of nations  we have a right and we need people to  help us  and get basic human rights  to all of our people because the  government isn't going to change  unless the voices of canadians and  indigenous people  speak up and make the government pause  and listen  and then change will come  next week on the new reality there is  just a  a lot of people that are succumbing to  false information because of covid and  us being in isolation a lot of people  are curious  they're alone so they're turning to  communities on the internet  people who tout conspiracy theory they  actually take advantage of people's  fears i don't feel it's wise to leave  these  people speaking unchecked it's  frightening it's  absolutely frightening  [Music]  that is the new reality for this week  i'm donna friesen  thanks for joining us  [Music]  you
 

